#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "d6a54cb2-ea48-57b1-bf2d-7aac09915159" :question/math-academy-id "e-8595" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,4,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,t,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,4,-t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & t & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 4 \\\\ 4 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & t \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}⟨(-{t}^{2}-16),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}⟨-({t}^{2}+16),0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "b2a775f5-4a27-5cba-931f-2a45db305152" :question/math-academy-id "e-8596" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point $t=\\pi$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩, \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\pi ) & =T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-0),-(0-4),(0-(-3))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "e805dcee-247b-5aa3-9856-f7bd48bd1a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-8597" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡4t+16{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16({cos}^{2}⁡4t+{sin}^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩}{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡4t-{cos}^{2}⁡4t),0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,1,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf8fcd4-bd10-5005-9fab-a36c2aa6be03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372009} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffd7cf1-5bfa-5ff5-b4f8-f45aa6934823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3-t},\\sqrt{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372010} {:answer/id #uuid "131db049-42d5-5505-9e07-8ec36d36dd62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372011} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4da52-e24f-5d58-8a9b-d4bae44ef6cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{3-t},\\sqrt{t},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372012} {:answer/id #uuid "d92fa104-4fb5-579f-9b80-ab815351fdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d92fa104-4fb5-579f-9b80-ab815351fdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372013} :answer-field/id #uuid "119d3a51-bb4f-5c27-9623-030c20c96853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "126d7092-f259-50a6-8d9e-e771713a98c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127613" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{t} & 0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0-0,-(t+(3-t)),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d609b4-1609-5857-9a5e-0e6b2ec15e31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372016} {:answer/id #uuid "c9adf763-534d-5d30-a79d-8740781c35e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372017} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbb3557-e907-531e-b418-c0f5c00df4ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372018} {:answer/id #uuid "4d404345-1056-50ef-97f1-ac415a3d0ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372019} {:answer/id #uuid "279ebc6b-ca4d-50f9-abba-dc2bf8621cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9adf763-534d-5d30-a79d-8740781c35e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372017} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e23b72-5b8e-5d8e-a84e-e2f50f12fe4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea055476-b8eb-519f-a665-b94bae5e3df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127616" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),\\sqrt{3},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),\\sqrt{3},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+2)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+2)),0-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "702d234c-3601-58ff-bc9e-b3f017f412fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+8{t}^{2}}}⟨2t,\\,2t,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372023} {:answer/id #uuid "ee633dc9-259f-5932-a7ac-b59005d2c22f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372024} {:answer/id #uuid "f82560b4-895a-5b07-aa98-c6e8551f1a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372025} {:answer/id #uuid "e90991c2-23b8-5f8e-9d2e-e9debfffad3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372026} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb5108c-048c-5bf8-90da-1a71c362297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fb5108c-048c-5bf8-90da-1a71c362297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a31ac0-ac45-5a52-9394-cc4a69ae0a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9708eb7-d471-5841-8ae9-c412fc9cab47" :question/math-academy-id "q-127686" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4t}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩}{(\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(1+4{t}^{2}),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,\\,-(1+4{t}^{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6ff39e-73f7-521c-84d8-7b27af6185c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2\\sin t, 2\\cos t, \\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166153} {:answer/id #uuid "da27ebe0-018f-59a3-8731-9a15ffc67a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\sin t, \\cos t, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166154} {:answer/id #uuid "0f76f5a7-9c09-5ec1-86e2-34d76c5f1f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨ - \\sin t,-\\cos t, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166155} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed35365-5029-5909-9a23-4a3a60609704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\sin t,-4\\cos t, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166156} {:answer/id #uuid "693ae0b8-4e90-5793-8816-1e0914161d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5}\\sin t, \\sqrt{5}\\cos t, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed35365-5029-5909-9a23-4a3a60609704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\sin t,-4\\cos t, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166156} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee32b5c-3991-5846-907c-c71944e46f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d801e9d7-50c3-5a8d-84f4-42fa2bf6b19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96957" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 4 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),-(0-(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f184a5eb-25ca-59de-a32b-ad9b5681218f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨ - 2, 4, \\sqrt{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166159} {:answer/id #uuid "f4442018-10b9-5cbb-b628-2ab656d49e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}⟨ - 1,-1, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166160} {:answer/id #uuid "292c3e97-93f7-5021-af2f-25b813cecf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166161} {:answer/id #uuid "55ed3e09-ee73-539d-befe-a05255f8fe16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨0, 4, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166162} {:answer/id #uuid "732a1eb0-a9b0-5369-b832-034168c9546b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}⟨ - 3, 2, \\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "292c3e97-93f7-5021-af2f-25b813cecf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166161} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca4e7b01-88cb-510d-a797-563a82c4c5d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5b8fb4d-5823-5562-9f22-dd9c5d6ae67a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96958" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨(-2-4),-(4+2),(4-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}⟨-6,-6,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb9c28b-277d-5e58-be8c-b1d02c360915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2, \\sqrt{2}\\sin t,-\\sqrt{2}\\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166165} {:answer/id #uuid "f3835411-f2b7-51c6-a18f-1ac2b78496c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1, 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin t,-2\\sqrt{2}\\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166166} {:answer/id #uuid "4251b206-1c65-5470-a26f-9b4e785e4f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,-2\\sin t, \\sqrt{2}\\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166167} {:answer/id #uuid "4523ae62-7cb6-5e20-a0a4-23f9a322c6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1, \\sin t, \\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166168} {:answer/id #uuid "5d797f58-df0b-573b-b3a2-25ac8eff66d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1, \\sqrt{2}\\sin t,-\\sqrt{2}\\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3835411-f2b7-51c6-a18f-1ac2b78496c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1, 2\\sqrt{2}\\sin t,-2\\sqrt{2}\\cos t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166166} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fbd7bea-939d-54db-95ae-06aff4685cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553afed7-053a-5b06-8cb8-cd7b8f67d1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96959" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c373dbd1-a7c4-573f-9560-44f00bd01ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372030} {:answer/id #uuid "903aba4b-e78a-5e03-830e-c3387527800e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372031} {:answer/id #uuid "783aa878-13d6-52c7-a483-87352235304a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨{e}^{t},0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} {:answer/id #uuid "067a1ae9-20c4-5ed0-bfec-2a58fbdf701b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372033} {:answer/id #uuid "82bf8855-8a26-52f3-9685-e5c8f9cfd5c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨{e}^{2t}+1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067a1ae9-20c4-5ed0-bfec-2a58fbdf701b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372033} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8404e0-34d1-52af-94e2-31704ce46c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9b6a500-d80c-58ab-8380-8435861bb646" :question/math-academy-id "q-99265" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,{e}^{t},1⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,1,-{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨0,1,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-{e}^{2t}-1,-(0-0),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}+1}⟨-({e}^{2t}+1),0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a6d0cc-9752-5f8c-afb8-a513184ab39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372037} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfb3298-d6a1-5704-9312-d0b555ebc5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372038} {:answer/id #uuid "8207d0eb-23be-51e4-ab8e-588f31698153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372039} {:answer/id #uuid "7f24520f-8e4b-5ef8-ba4a-9dfc7a39fa2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372040} {:answer/id #uuid "34687163-daf0-5df2-beb7-29c36f58f6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f24520f-8e4b-5ef8-ba4a-9dfc7a39fa2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba408c5-cc53-5e68-b929-b9484f55411b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4efd96a2-69d1-56ab-9a41-2279eca34149" :question/math-academy-id "q-99413" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1) & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1) & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1)-0,-({sin}^{2}⁡(2t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(2t-1)),0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-1),-1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8048a3c3-52ec-500f-bcef-23728084b27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372044} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0f038c-62f3-5f95-84bd-4365347af185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372045} {:answer/id #uuid "e963946e-be1d-5014-868c-4a726d521214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372046} {:answer/id #uuid "603ca2b0-1134-5336-8800-592361364268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372047} {:answer/id #uuid "27b8c3dd-572c-5e2a-b80d-68510104726d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603ca2b0-1134-5336-8800-592361364268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372047} :answer-field/id #uuid "874a5266-d069-5a40-8d2e-d7cb0d835313" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a233a198-a68f-5820-8551-5bd5e6e915ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-99415" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,5,4⟩, \\\\ N(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =T(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\times N(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,5,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨(-15-0),-(-9-16),(0-20)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨-15,25,-20⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b3d4385-e521-54e8-9f27-92b12645a56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372051} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e0df4d-f18b-5442-bee5-8f331c67a9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372052} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5db06-db05-5619-921c-9a28019c6674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372053} {:answer/id #uuid "f78ee6a1-a996-5733-83f6-8b374c59e4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372054} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3762ad-da00-506d-8570-7149b0f61703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b3d4385-e521-54e8-9f27-92b12645a56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372051} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b33378-f1f7-5241-8734-a321e15da398" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d72357b6-ef82-52db-9e20-719b16aff0ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-99420" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩, \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨(-4-5),-(-4-(-4)),(10-(-8))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨-9,0,18⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8f027a-bdec-570e-9b2d-0b1a6ce50144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372058} {:answer/id #uuid "f314dfaa-e9a0-5e3b-bfd7-7866a52236b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨-1,1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372059} {:answer/id #uuid "d34bc728-0722-5dc4-81cc-ed1f251353ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372060} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c9c7b2-14ab-5370-8f52-f663f1f8a2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨\\sqrt{2},-5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372061} {:answer/id #uuid "318bc00b-947f-5c1f-ba16-2b80a8c12e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{3}}⟨3,5\\sqrt{2},4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8f027a-bdec-570e-9b2d-0b1a6ce50144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372058} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda6b27e-9695-5d30-8cae-3f262d163f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5570a23-d111-5812-8b43-05d4d1f22b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-99429" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩, \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨(2\\sqrt{2}-(-\\sqrt{2})),-(-2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}),(1-3)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2},-(-5\\sqrt{2}),-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{12}⟨3\\sqrt{2},5\\sqrt{2},-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨3,5,-\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6c4a6ce-313d-50a9-b549-162f2135a26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨4,-1,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372065} {:answer/id #uuid "d0579e7b-ae94-5ae3-81eb-b79b3f2aa2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372066} {:answer/id #uuid "98dc35fe-a3a6-51e3-a375-357bd51a1c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372067} {:answer/id #uuid "7b14be0e-f45c-5f2e-af2b-9c0d755afc08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372068} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc81bb0-2667-5fe0-a54d-b873bd2bb008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0579e7b-ae94-5ae3-81eb-b79b3f2aa2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372066} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20313f1-97c0-5fc8-8b64-20fda22d54f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e302d40d-778b-5d69-b79d-6c911b05e594" :question/math-academy-id "q-99430" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩, \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3},-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨(1+3),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d21ad4e3-4e8d-5160-8f7e-14708fcd74c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}}⟨ - 2, t, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166405} {:answer/id #uuid "d3761073-5b4f-5b81-8819-8784f4f2c469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}⟨0, 1, t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166406} {:answer/id #uuid "dc134c18-0277-5cf2-9869-346e1c845030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1, 0, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166407} {:answer/id #uuid "130e2e03-4140-5166-982d-9744a243c698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1, 1, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166408} {:answer/id #uuid "758c15f2-4a2e-5fb1-8073-7582beee784c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758c15f2-4a2e-5fb1-8073-7582beee784c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0, 0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166409} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7cf5d3f-883e-56cd-b492-c81be00a357b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38a9961c-14c0-5e12-92b6-0fd05f67f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-99504" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f61ff981-79e6-5ee2-b177-d3b9b017bb19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372072} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8815dc-4d18-5cd2-ba78-3c8553a1ca2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372073} {:answer/id #uuid "ea05a089-d7a0-56fc-be98-b597f99cd0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372074} {:answer/id #uuid "09c99773-42be-5d73-a2b2-3cd11cae9247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372075} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d61bc8-7f50-5d75-9c14-98ed028f78c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c99773-42be-5d73-a2b2-3cd11cae9247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372075} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a5929a-15f7-5ad9-b4f5-2944f1ea41dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3acb097-24af-576e-b081-d26bf643475d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99574" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0,-(-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}),-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a09701-fbc0-531a-b9b6-cbe979759d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372079} {:answer/id #uuid "48af7b6a-f57b-5bd2-be7d-fd4b26b6bb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372080} {:answer/id #uuid "3a812dbd-c7be-50a6-b715-546dd1cf91b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372081} {:answer/id #uuid "35b999fb-8ef9-581e-9f5f-9b833658a4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372082} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c1e693-a8d6-5651-ab56-de4383395389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35b999fb-8ef9-581e-9f5f-9b833658a4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372082} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2663263-0ee6-5e60-b6b3-d27a774b454b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "780d7d73-3673-560c-aa7d-955afb9a9935" :question/math-academy-id "q-99653" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
